&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Purpose.&lt;/strong&gt; Be the person who actually fixes the robot, and writes down how.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Responsibilities&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Take robot issues from L1 and monitoring; triage, diagnose and resolve.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Work directly on robots: read logs, inspect ROS topics and services, interact with CAN, check and change configuration, disable sensors where safe, restart services; request a version rollback from V&amp;amp;V when a release is implicated.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Investigate connectivity problems, mapping and annotation issues, tower faults, and mission or deployment failures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Know when to stop. Escalate to L3 or Engineering with a clean, evidenced handover - what you saw, what you tried, what you ruled out.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write and maintain a playbook for every repeat issue you resolve, so the next person does not start from scratch.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Train and support L1, Deployment and Service so fewer issues need to reach you; keep the knowledge centre and post-release digest current.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Feed recurring problems to Fleet Reliability so they are fixed permanently rather than handled repeatedly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You&#39;ll need&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A technical degree in engineering, robotics, computer science or similar, or equivalent hands-on experience; robotics exposure welcome but not essential.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Linux and command-line confidence; willingness to learn ROS quickly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A structured troubleshooting instinct: form a hypothesis, test it, discard it, move on.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Calm under operational pressure - robots fail at inconvenient times and people will be waiting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clear written communication - the playbook matters as much as the fix.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
